  "sources": [
   "<li>J. Welles Wilder Jr., <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/newconceptsintec00wild\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">New Concepts in Technical Trading Systems</a> (Trend Research, Greensboro NC, 1978; ISBN 0-89459-027-8), via the Internet Archive &mdash; title page, copyright and the table of contents listing Section VI, &ldquo;The Relative Strength Index.&rdquo; The book is the origin of RSI. Note: the scanned copy is lending-restricted, and Section VI itself could not be read for this page.</li>",
   "<li>J. Welles Wilder Jr., <a href=\"https://store.traders.com/-v04-c09-rsi-pdf.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">&ldquo;The Relative Strength Index&rdquo;</a>, <em>Technical Analysis of STOCKS &amp; COMMODITIES</em> V.4:9 (December 1986), pp.343&ndash;346 &mdash; publisher&rsquo;s abstract of Wilder&rsquo;s own bylined article, which is the source for the 70/30 tops-and-bottoms rule and for his five listed uses: tops and bottoms, chart formations on the RSI line, failure swings, support and resistance, and divergence.</li>",
   "<li>TradingView, <a href=\"https://www.tradingview.com/support/solutions/43000502338-relative-strength-index-rsi/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Relative Strength Index (RSI)</a> &mdash; official platform documentation giving the formula RSI = 100 &minus; 100 &divide; (1 + RS) with RS as average gain over average loss, the 0 to 100 scale, &ldquo;14 bars is the default,&rdquo; and the attribution of 70/30 to Wilder&rsquo;s 1978 book.</li>",
   "<li>StockCharts.com, <a href=\"https://chartschool.stockcharts.com/table-of-contents/technical-indicators-and-overlays/technical-indicators/relative-strength-index-rsi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ChartSchool: Relative Strength Index (RSI)</a> &mdash; the identical formula, the 14-period default, 70/30, the rendering of Constance Brown&rsquo;s ranges as roughly 40&ndash;90 in a bull market and 10&ndash;60 in a bear market, and the attribution of positive and negative reversals to Andrew Cardwell. Also checked for a LeBeau attribution, which it does not contain.</li>",
   "<li>Charles Schwab &amp; Co. / thinkorswim Learning Center, <a href=\"https://toslc.thinkorswim.com/center/reference/Tech-Indicators/studies-library/R-S/RSI\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">RSI study reference</a> &mdash; describes RSI as rating strength on a scale from 0 to 100 and ships default levels of 30 oversold and 70 overbought, verified as current.</li>",
   "<li>CMT Association, <a href=\"https://cmtassociation.org/chartadvisor/mastering-the-relative-strength-index-rsi-how-to-read-it-correctly/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mastering the Relative Strength Index (RSI): How to Read it Correctly</a>, 9 May 2025 &mdash; the professional body for technical analysts, stating Brown&rsquo;s ranges as uptrend lows of 40&ndash;50 with highs of 80&ndash;90 and downtrend lows of 20&ndash;30 with highs of 55&ndash;65. Cited here because it conflicts with the StockCharts rendering of the same author.</li>",
   "<li>Constance M. Brown, <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/technicalanalysi00brow\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Technical Analysis for the Trading Professional</a> (McGraw-Hill, 1999; ISBN 0-07-012062-5) &mdash; the primary source that would settle the conflicting renderings of her RSI ranges. The Internet Archive item is flagged access-restricted and could not be read for this page. Listed so the gap is on the record.</li>",
   "<li>Charles LeBeau and David W. Lucas, <em>Technical Traders Guide to Computer Analysis of the Futures Markets</em> (Irwin, 1992; ISBN 1-55623-468-6) &mdash; the primary source that would support the 75/25 levels commonly attributed to LeBeau. No copy was reachable and no accessible text attributes those levels to him, which is why this page treats 75/25 as unsourced convention rather than as a rule.</li>"
  ],
