  "next": [
   "<li>If a death has just happened, start with <a href=\"../death-of-a-spouse.html\">the death of a spouse guide</a> &mdash; survivor benefits, joint debt and the order the paperwork has to go in. Free, no account.</li>",
   "<li>Work through <a href=\"../stage-5-wealth.html\">Stage 5 &middot; Build Wealth</a>, where estate basics sit next to taxes, housing and income streams.</li>",
   "<li>Put a number on the tax at stake with the <a href=\"../step-up-in-basis-calculator.html\">step-up in basis calculator</a>.</li>",
   "<li>If you have inherited retirement accounts and now have to draw them down, read <a href=\"../retirement-withdrawal.html\">the retirement withdrawal guide</a>.</li>",
   "<li>If collectors are calling about accounts that are genuinely yours, <a href=\"../after-financial-collapse.html\">the after-collapse guide</a> covers validation and what they can and cannot do.</li>",
   "<li>Browse every definition in <a href=\"../learn-the-lingo.html\">Learn the Lingo</a>.</li>"
  ],
  "sources": [
   "<li>Federal Trade Commission, <a href=\"https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/debts-and-deceased-relatives\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Debts and Deceased Relatives</a> &mdash; that debts are owed by and paid from the estate, that family members usually do not pay from their own money, and the short list of situations creating personal liability.</li>",
   "<li>Consumer Financial Protection Bureau, <a href=\"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/does-a-persons-debt-go-away-when-they-die-en-1463/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Does a person's debt go away when they die?</a> &mdash; the statement that a debt an estate cannot pay generally goes unpaid, and the community property state list.</li>",
   "<li>Consumer Financial Protection Bureau, <a href=\"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/rules-policy/regulations/1006/6/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Regulation F, 12 CFR &sect; 1006.6 &mdash; Communications in connection with debt collection</a> &mdash; the closed list in &sect; 1006.6(a) of who counts as the &ldquo;consumer&rdquo; a collector may discuss a debt with, including the executor or administrator of a deceased consumer's estate.</li>",
   "<li>Consumer Financial Protection Bureau, <a href=\"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/i-was-an-authorized-user-on-my-deceased-relatives-credit-card-account-am-i-liable-to-repay-the-debt-en-1485/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Authorized user liability on a deceased relative's card</a> &mdash; that an authorized user generally is not obligated to pay the debt.</li>",
   "<li>Cornell Legal Information Institute, <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/uniform_probate_code\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Uniform Probate Code</a> &mdash; the model act's role and the count of 18 states adopting it at least in part, which is the basis for this page's refusal to quote national rules.</li>",
   "<li>Minnesota Office of the Revisor of Statutes, <a href=\"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/statutes/cite/524.3-803\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Minn. Stat. &sect; 524.3-803, Limitations on presentation of claims</a> &mdash; the four-month window after published notice, the one-month-after-service alternative, and the one-year bar measured from the date of death.</li>",
   "<li>Maine Legislature, <a href=\"https://legislature.maine.gov/statutes/18-C/title18-Csec6-102.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">18-C M.R.S. &sect; 6-102, Liability of nonprobate transferees for creditor claims and statutory allowances</a> &mdash; that avoiding probate does not automatically put an asset beyond the estate's creditors in a state with this provision, and the carve-out for a survivorship interest in joint real estate.</li>",
   "<li>National Conference of State Legislatures, <a href=\"https://www.ncsl.org/resources/map-monday-states-spell-out-when-adult-children-have-a-duty-to-care-for-parents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">States Spell Out When Adult Children Have a Duty to Care for Parents</a> &mdash; the count of 27 states with filial responsibility laws and the statement that they are rarely invoked.</li>",
   "<li>Judicial Council of California, <a href=\"https://courts.ca.gov/sites/default/files/courts/default/2024-11/de300.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Form DE-300, Maximum Values for Small Estate Set-Aside and Dispositions Without Administration</a> &mdash; the $208,850 affidavit limit and $107,900 set-aside limit for deaths on or after April 1, 2025, and the prior figures.</li>"
  ],
