   "<li>Cornell Legal Information Institute, <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/4974\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">26 U.S.C. &sect; 4974 &mdash; Excise tax on certain accumulations</a> &mdash; the 25% tax in (a), the waiver for reasonable error in (d), and the reduction to 10% and the definition of the correction window in (e).</li>",
   "<li>Cornell Legal Information Institute, <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/26/1.401(a)(9)-9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">26 CFR &sect; 1.401(a)(9)-9 &mdash; Life expectancy and distribution period tables</a> &mdash; the Single Life Table in (b), the Uniform Lifetime Table in (c) and the Joint and Last Survivor Table in (d), and every factor quoted on this page.</li>",
   "<li>Electronic Code of Federal Regulations, <a href=\"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-26/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-1/subject-group-ECFR6f8c3724b50e44d/section-1.401(a)(9)-2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">26 CFR &sect; 1.401(a)(9)-2 &mdash; Distributions commencing during an employee's lifetime</a> &mdash; the applicable age by birth-year cohort in (b)(2), including the paragraph for employees born in 1959, which currently reads [Reserved].</li>",
   "<li>Federal Register, <a href=\"https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2024/07/19/2024-14543/required-minimum-distributions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Required Minimum Distributions, proposed rule, 19 July 2024</a> &mdash; proposed &sect; 1.401(a)(9)-2(b)(2)(v), which would provide that an employee born in 1959 has an applicable age of 73.</li>",
   "<li>Federal Register, <a href=\"https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2024/07/19/2024-14542/required-minimum-distributions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Required Minimum Distributions, final rule, 19 July 2024</a> &mdash; confirmation that the life expectancy tables remain those published 12 November 2020 and applicable from 2022, the annual-distribution requirement inside the ten-year window, and the general 1 January 2025 applicability date.</li>",
   "<li>Internal Revenue Service, <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/retirement-plan-and-ira-required-minimum-distributions-faqs\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Retirement plan and IRA required minimum distributions FAQs</a> &mdash; the aggregation rules for IRAs, 403(b) contracts and 401(k) and 457(b) plans, the still-working exception and the 5% owner limit, the 25% and 10% excise tax figures, and the Form 5329 filing instruction.</li>",
   "<li>Internal Revenue Service, <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Publication 590-B, Distributions from Individual Retirement Arrangements (IRAs)</a> &mdash; the reader-facing document, and Appendix B where Table I (Single Life), Table II (Joint and Last Survivor) and Table III (Uniform Lifetime) are reprinted.</li>",
   "<li>Internal Revenue Service, <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/n-25-67.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Notice 2025-67, 2026 amounts relating to retirement plans and IRAs</a> &mdash; the &sect; 408(d)(8) qualified charitable distribution limit rising from $108,000 to $111,000 for 2026, and the one-time split-interest entity limit of $55,000.</li>"
