  "termDescription": "A sinking fund is money set aside on a schedule to pay for a known future expense, so the cost is spread over the months before it arrives instead of landing all at once. The term is borrowed from bond finance, where a sinking fund provision requires an issuer to set money aside on a schedule to retire debt rather than face the whole principal at maturity. In personal finance it is a budgeting convention, not a regulated product: the arithmetic is total cost divided by the number of months until the expense is due.",
