  "sources": [
   "<li>John Bollinger, <a href=\"https://www.bollingerbands.com/bollinger-band-rules\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">22 Bollinger Bands Rules</a> &mdash; the originator&rsquo;s own published rule list, and the source for every rule quoted verbatim on this page: Rule 1 (a relative definition of high and low), Rule 2, Rule 4, Rule 6 (tags are not signals), Rule 7 (walking the bands), Rule 8 (closes outside are initially continuation signals), Rule 9 (20 and 2 are defaults), Rule 11 (2.1 at 50 periods), Rule 12 (why a simple average), Rule 14 (make no statistical assumptions; 90% not 95%), Rules 15 and 17 (%b), Rules 18 and 19 (BandWidth and the Squeeze), and Rule 22 (no continuous advice).</li>",
   "<li>John Bollinger, <a href=\"https://www.bollingerbands.com/bollinger-bands\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bollinger Bands</a> &mdash; his construction notes: a middle band moving average with upper and lower bands answering whether price is high or low on a relative basis, defaults of 20 periods and plus or minus two standard deviations unchanged since the beginning, explicit use of the <b>population</b> standard deviation calculation, and his account of adding %b and BandWidth afterwards.</li>",
   "<li>TradingView, <a href=\"https://www.tradingview.com/support/solutions/43000501971-bollinger-bands-bb/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bollinger Bands (BB)</a> &mdash; official platform documentation confirming the 20-day simple moving average middle line, 20 as the default length and 2 as the default deviation multiplier, the %b formula, and the statement that during strong trends breakthroughs of the bands &ldquo;are not actual reversal signals&rdquo; and the genuine turn &ldquo;can be a difficult event to pinpoint.&rdquo;</li>",
   "<li>StockCharts.com, <a href=\"https://chartschool.stockcharts.com/table-of-contents/technical-indicators-and-overlays/technical-overlays/bollinger-bands\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ChartSchool: Bollinger Bands</a> &mdash; the identical construction (20-day SMA, plus and minus two 20-day standard deviations) and the claim that the bands &ldquo;should contain 88-89% of price action,&rdquo; cited here because it disagrees with Bollinger&rsquo;s own 90% figure and neither publishes its data.</li>",
   "<li>StockCharts.com, <a href=\"https://chartschool.stockcharts.com/table-of-contents/technical-indicators-and-overlays/technical-indicators/bollinger-bandwidth\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ChartSchool: Bollinger BandWidth</a> &mdash; the BandWidth formula ( ( upper &minus; lower ) &divide; middle ) &times; 100, the definition of the Squeeze as volatility falling to a low level, the theory that low-volatility periods are followed by high-volatility periods, the instruction to gauge BandWidth against a security&rsquo;s own prior eight to twelve months rather than a fixed threshold, and the warning that initial breaks can fail.</li>",
   "<li>Eugene F. Fama, <a href=\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/The-Behavior-of-Stock-Market-Prices-Fama/d4bd6df2ba1df56677097ef8fc09a72b74ade910\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">&ldquo;The Behavior of Stock-Market Prices&rdquo;</a>, <em>The Journal of Business</em> 38(1), January 1965, pp. 34&ndash;105 &mdash; the named study behind this page&rsquo;s statement that daily stock returns have fatter tails and a taller peak than a normal distribution, which is why the two-standard-deviation intuition does not transfer to a price chart.</li>",
   "<li>Robert F. Engle, <a href=\"https://www.econometricsociety.org/publications/econometrica/1982/07/01/autoregressive-conditional-heteroscedasticity-estimates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">&ldquo;Autoregressive Conditional Heteroscedasticity with Estimates of the Variance of United Kingdom Inflation&rdquo;</a>, <em>Econometrica</em> 50(4), 1982 &mdash; the origin of ARCH modeling and the named source for volatility clustering, the second reason a single rolling standard deviation is not describing a stable spread.</li>",
   "<li>The Nobel Prize, <a href=\"https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/economic-sciences/2003/engle/facts/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robert F. Engle III &mdash; Facts, 2003 Prize in Economic Sciences</a> &mdash; the official citation wording quoted on this page, &ldquo;for methods of analyzing economic time series with time-varying volatility (ARCH).&rdquo;</li>"
  ],
