  "sources": [
   "<li>StockCharts.com, <a href=\"https://chartschool.stockcharts.com/table-of-contents/technical-indicators-and-overlays/technical-overlays/moving-averages-simple-and-exponential\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ChartSchool: Moving Averages &mdash; Simple and Exponential</a> &mdash; the source for the SMA definition and its 5-day rolling example, the three-step EMA calculation, the multiplier formula 2 &divide; (time periods + 1) with 18.18% for 10 periods and 9.52% for 20, the statement that exponential averages have less lag and turn before simple ones, the golden cross and death cross definitions, the whipsaw warning on crossover systems, the popularity of the 50-day and 200-day, and the line that trading ranges render moving averages ineffective.</li>",
   "<li>TradingView, <a href=\"https://www.tradingview.com/support/solutions/43000502589-moving-average/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moving Average</a> &mdash; official platform documentation describing the indicator as &ldquo;price based, lagging (or reactive),&rdquo; stating that it &ldquo;is not used as a predictive indicator but rather an interpretive one, used for confirmations and analysis,&rdquo; noting that longer timeframes carry more lag, and giving the study's own default length of 9.</li>",
   "<li>William Brock, Josef Lakonishok and Blake LeBaron, <a href=\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1540-6261.1992.tb04681.x\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">&ldquo;Simple Technical Trading Rules and the Stochastic Properties of Stock Returns&rdquo;</a>, <em>Journal of Finance</em> 47(5), 1992, pp.&nbsp;1731&ndash;1764 &mdash; the study of 26 technical rules, including moving-average rules, on daily Dow Jones Industrial Average data from 1897 to 1986. This is the paper behind most claims that moving-average rules have been shown to work.</li>",
   "<li>Ryan Sullivan, Allan Timmermann and Halbert White, <a href=\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/0022-1082.00163\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">&ldquo;Data-Snooping, Technical Trading Rule Performance, and the Bootstrap&rdquo;</a>, <em>Journal of Finance</em> 54(5), 1999, pp.&nbsp;1647&ndash;1691 &mdash; expands the Brock, Lakonishok and LeBaron rule universe, applies White's Reality Check bootstrap to 100 years of Dow Jones data, finds that some rules outperformed in the 1897&ndash;1986 window &ldquo;even after adjustment is made for data-snooping,&rdquo; and reports &ldquo;scant evidence that technical trading rules were of any economic value during the period 1987&ndash;1996,&rdquo; with the best rule's probability of failing to beat the benchmark in that period at &ldquo;nearly 12 percent.&rdquo; The source for every statement on this page about whether these rules work.</li>",
   "<li>TradingView, <a href=\"https://www.tradingview.com/symbols/INDEX-S5TH/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">S&amp;P 500 Stocks Above 200-Day Average (INDEX:S5TH)</a> &mdash; the published breadth index used on this site's technical page, confirming that the series measures the percentage of S&amp;P 500 constituents trading above their 200-day moving average. Its 20-day and 50-day siblings are S5TW and S5FI.</li>",
   "<li>Hustlin', <a href=\"../markets-technical.html\">Markets &middot; Technical</a> &mdash; this site's own implementation, and the source for the stated limit that arbitrary lookback periods cannot be embedded in a free charting widget and would have to be recreated inside a charting account. The 20, 50 and 200-day participation indices are used because they exist as published series with long histories.</li>",
   "<li>William Gordon, <a href=\"https://books.google.com/books/about/The_stock_market_indicators.html?id=rk8UAQAAMAAJ\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><em>The Stock Market Indicators: As a Guide to Market Timing</em></a> (Investors' Press, 1968) &mdash; the book most often named as the early testing of long-window moving averages, and the source that would settle where the 200-day convention came from. No readable copy was reachable for this page, which is why the ledger above marks that origin unverified rather than repeating the usual attribution.</li>"
  ],
