  "termDescription": "A stop-loss order is an instruction to a brokerage firm to buy or sell a security once its price reaches a specified stop price. When the stop price is reached, a plain stop order becomes a market order and is executed at whatever price is then available, so the stop price is a trigger rather than a guaranteed execution price. A stop-limit order instead becomes a limit order at a named price, which protects the price but may not execute at all.",
