  "termDescription": "Dividend yield is a company's annual dividends per share divided by its current share price, expressed as a percentage. Because the share price is the denominator, the yield rises when the price falls, so a high yield can signal a marked-down stock rather than a generous one. Unlike a bank yield, a dividend is declared at the discretion of the board and can be cut or eliminated at any time.",
