  "sources": [
   "<li>U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission, Division of Corporation Finance, <a href=\"https://www.sec.gov/rules-regulations/staff-guidance/corporation-finance-interpretations/non-gaap-financial-measures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Non-GAAP Financial Measures Compliance &amp; Disclosure Interpretations</a> &mdash; Question 102.07 is the spine of this page: that free cash flow is &ldquo;typically calculated as cash flows from operating activities&hellip; less capital expenditures,&rdquo; that &ldquo;this measure does not have a uniform definition and its title does not describe how it is calculated,&rdquo; that it must not imply residual cash available for discretionary expenditures given mandatory debt service, and that it &ldquo;must not be presented on a per share basis.&rdquo;</li>",
   "<li>17 CFR Part 244, <a href=\"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-17/chapter-II/part-244/section-244.100\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Regulation G, &sect;&nbsp;244.100</a> &mdash; source for the requirement to present the most directly comparable GAAP measure and &ldquo;a reconciliation (by schedule or other clearly understandable method),&rdquo; and for the prohibition in paragraph (b) on a non-GAAP measure that contains an untrue statement of material fact or omits one necessary to keep the presentation from being misleading.</li>",
   "<li>17 CFR &sect;&nbsp;229.10(e), <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/17/229.10\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Item 10(e) of Regulation S-K</a> &mdash; source for the definition of a non-GAAP financial measure at (e)(2), the equal-or-greater-prominence requirement, the statement of why management finds the measure useful, and the express carve-out at (e)(1)(ii) for &ldquo;the measures earnings before interest and taxes (EBIT) and earnings before interest, taxes, depreciation, and amortization (EBITDA).&rdquo;</li>",
   "<li>Financial Accounting Standards Board, <a href=\"https://asc.fasb.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Accounting Standards Codification Topic 230, Statement of Cash Flows</a>; the quoted capital-expenditure language at ASC 230-10-45-13 is reproduced in <a href=\"https://storage.fasb.org/ASU%202016-15.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Accounting Standards Update 2016-15</a> &mdash; source for the three-section structure of the statement, the indirect method, and the investing outflow for &ldquo;payments at the time of purchase or soon before or after purchase to acquire property, plant, and equipment and other productive assets, including interest capitalized as part of the cost of those assets.&rdquo;</li>",
   "<li>17 CFR &sect;&nbsp;210.3-02, <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/17/210.3-02\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Regulation S-X Rule 3-02</a> &mdash; establishes that audited statements of cash flows are filed for each of the three fiscal years preceding the most recent audited balance sheet, which is why one 10-K gives three years of both inputs.</li>",
   "<li>U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission, <a href=\"https://www.sec.gov/edgar/search/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EDGAR full-text search</a> &mdash; the free, primary route to any US filer's statement of cash flows and to the non-GAAP reconciliation in its earnings release. This is where a free cash flow figure is actually checkable.</li>",
   "<li>U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission, <a href=\"https://www.sec.gov/interps/account/sabcodet14.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Staff Accounting Bulletin Codification Topic 14: Share-Based Payment</a> &mdash; source for the requirement that &ldquo;compensation cost resulting from share-based payment transactions be recognized in financial statements at fair value&rdquo; under the FASB's stock compensation standard, which is what makes stock compensation an expense that is nevertheless non-cash.</li>",
   "<li>Michael J. Mauboussin and Dan Callahan, <a href=\"https://www.morganstanley.com/im/publication/insights/articles/article_stockbasedcompensation.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">&ldquo;Stock-Based Compensation: Unpacking the Issues&rdquo;</a>, Morgan Stanley Counterpoint Global, 18 April 2023 &mdash; source for the quoted statement that &ldquo;cash flow from operating activities does not reflect SBC as an expense,&rdquo; and for their recommendation to treat it first as an expense reducing operating cash flow and second as equity raised.</li>",
   "<li>Aswath Damodaran, <a href=\"https://aswathdamodaran.substack.com/p/earnings-cash-flows-and-free-cash\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">&ldquo;Earnings, Cash Flows and Free Cash Flows: A Primer&rdquo;</a>, 31 October 2022 &mdash; the other named side of the stock-compensation argument, and source for the quoted description of free cash flow as &ldquo;one of the most dangerous terms in finance.&rdquo; Cited as an attributable position, not as an authority.</li>"
  ],
