   "<li>Legal Information Institute, Cornell Law School &mdash; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">26 U.S.C. &sect; 1(h), Maximum capital gains rate</a> &mdash; establishes that the preferential rate is applied to tiers of taxable income, which is why a gain can straddle two rates.</li>",
   "<li>Internal Revenue Service &mdash; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/rp-25-32.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rev. Proc. 2025-32, inflation adjustments for tax year 2026 (PDF)</a> &mdash; establishes every 2026 figure on this page: the 0% and 15% breakpoints by filing status, the estate and trust breakpoints, and the $16,100 / $32,200 standard deductions.</li>",
   "<li>Internal Revenue Service &mdash; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc409\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Topic No. 409, Capital gains and losses</a> &mdash; establishes the 28% maximum collectibles rate, the 25% maximum unrecaptured section 1250 rate, and the $3,000 / $1,500 loss deduction. Displayed 2025 bracket figures when checked on 12 August 2026.</li>",
   "<li>Legal Information Institute, Cornell Law School &mdash; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/1411\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">26 U.S.C. &sect; 1411, Imposition of tax</a> &mdash; establishes the 3.8 percent rate, the $250,000 / $200,000 threshold amounts, and the absence of any inflation adjustment clause.</li>",
   "<li>Internal Revenue Service &mdash; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/individuals/net-investment-income-tax\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Net Investment Income Tax</a> &mdash; establishes the thresholds by filing status and that capital gains are included in net investment income.</li>",
   "<li>Legal Information Institute, Cornell Law School &mdash; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/121\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">26 U.S.C. &sect; 121, Exclusion of gain from sale of principal residence</a> &mdash; establishes the two-of-five-year test, the $250,000 and $500,000 limits, the two-year lookout rule, the surviving spouse rule in (b)(4), the nonqualified use allocation in (b)(5), and the divorce use rule in (d)(3)(B).</li>",
   "<li>Internal Revenue Service &mdash; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc701\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Topic No. 701, Sale of your home</a> &mdash; establishes the 24-month ownership and use framing and the Form 1099-S reporting requirement even when gain is excluded.</li>",
   "<li>Legal Information Institute, Cornell Law School &mdash; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/1211\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">26 U.S.C. &sect; 1211, Limitation on capital losses</a> &mdash; establishes the quoted $3,000 / $1,500 language and that losses offset gains without limit before the cap applies.</li>",
   "<li>Legal Information Institute, Cornell Law School &mdash; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/1212\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">26 U.S.C. &sect; 1212, Capital loss carrybacks and carryovers</a> &mdash; establishes that a carried-forward loss retains its short-term or long-term character and that no expiration is stated for individuals.</li>",
   "<li>Legal Information Institute, Cornell Law School &mdash; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/1014\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">26 U.S.C. &sect; 1014, Basis of property acquired from a decedent</a> &mdash; establishes the date-of-death basis reset referenced in the inherited-house examples.</li>",
   "<li>Internal Revenue Service &mdash; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-publication-523\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">About Publication 523, Selling Your Home</a> &mdash; the IRS worksheets for computing adjusted basis and excluded gain on a residence; listed as the place to work the home numbers, not quoted here.</li>",
   "<li>Internal Revenue Service &mdash; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-schedule-d-form-1040\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">About Schedule D (Form 1040)</a> &mdash; the form on which the netting order and the carryforward described on this page are actually performed.</li>"
