  "termDescription": "Wage garnishment is a legal process in which an employer is ordered to withhold part of an employee&rsquo;s earnings and pay it to a creditor, a government agency, or a person owed support. In the United States the outer limit for ordinary consumer debts is set by Title III of the Consumer Credit Protection Act, 15 U.S.C. &sect;&sect; 1671&ndash;1677, which caps withholding at the lesser of 25 percent of disposable earnings or the amount by which weekly disposable earnings exceed thirty times the federal minimum hourly wage. Higher ceilings apply to child support and alimony, and separate federal schemes govern federal student loans and Internal Revenue Service levies. State law may protect more of a worker&rsquo;s pay, and where it does, the state rule controls.",
