  "termDescription": "The wash sale rule is a provision of United States federal income tax law, at 26 U.S.C. &sect; 1091, that disallows a loss deduction when a taxpayer sells stock or securities at a loss and acquires substantially identical stock or securities within a period beginning 30 days before the sale and ending 30 days after it. The disallowed loss is generally not forfeited: under &sect; 1091(d) it is added to the cost basis of the replacement securities, and under &sect; 1223(3) the replacement inherits the original holding period. The loss is permanently lost, however, when the replacement is purchased inside the taxpayer's own individual retirement account or Roth IRA, because there is no taxable basis to increase. As of August 2026 the rule reaches stock or securities only, not digital assets.",
